---
import Shell from "../../layouts/Shell.astro"
import { catalog } from "../../../src/model/catalog.ts"
import { buildDomains } from "../../../src/model/domains.ts"

/** Counts read as prose here, and one of them is genuinely 1. */
const count = (n: number, one: string, many = `${one}s`) => (n > 0 ? `${n} ${n === 1 ? one : many}` : undefined)

const data = await catalog()
const domains = buildDomains(data).map((domain) => ({
	...domain,
	signals: domain.spans.length + domain.events.length + domain.entities.length,
}))

const total = domains.reduce((n, domain) => n + domain.attributes.length, 0)
---

<Shell
	title="Domains"
	description="The OpenTelemetry conventions by topic: databases, HTTP, messaging, Kubernetes, GenAI and more, each pulling together its attributes, metrics, spans and the specification rules that bind them."
>
	<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
		<h1 class="m-0 text-[1.7rem] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">Domains</h1>
		<span class="text-[13.5px] text-faint">as of v{data.semconv.latest.version}</span>
	</div>
	<p class="mt-2 mb-0 text-muted max-w-[66ch]">
		The conventions ship as a flat registry, but nobody arrives asking what is in the registry &mdash;
		they arrive asking about databases, or about HTTP. Each domain below gathers one topic's
		attributes, metrics and signals from both registries, and points at the part of the specification
		that binds them. {total} attributes, in {domains.length} topics.
	</p>

	<div class="mt-8 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-7 sm:grid-cols-2">
		{
			domains.map((domain) => (
				// `min-w-0`: the namespace line below is `truncate`, and a grid item
				// defaults to min-width:auto, so without this the nowrap text widens
				// the track and the whole page scrolls sideways on a phone.
				<article class="min-w-0">
					<h2 class="m-0 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2.5 text-[15px] font-semibold tracking-tight">
						<a href={`/domains/${domain.slug}`} class="no-underline hover:underline">
							{domain.title}
						</a>
						<span class="font-mono text-[12.5px] font-normal text-faint">{domain.attributes.length} attributes</span>
					</h2>
					<p class="mt-1 mb-0 font-mono text-[12px] text-muted truncate">
						{domain.namespaces
							.slice(0, 5)
							.map((ns) => ns.name)
							.join(" · ")}
						{domain.namespaces.length > 5 && ` · +${domain.namespaces.length - 5}`}
					</p>
					<p class="mt-2 mb-0 text-[13.5px] text-muted">{domain.blurb}</p>
					<p class="mt-1.5 mb-0 text-[12.5px] text-faint">
						{[
							count(domain.metrics.length, "metric"),
							count(domain.spans.length, "span"),
							count(domain.events.length, "event"),
							count(domain.entities.length, "entity", "entities"),
							domain.deprecated > 0 && `${domain.deprecated} deprecated`,
						]
							.filter(Boolean)
							.join(" · ")}
					</p>
				</article>
			))
		}
	</div>

	<p class="mt-12 mb-0 text-[13px] text-faint max-w-[66ch]">
		Grouping is the one editorial judgement on this site: upstream defines the namespaces, not the
		topics they belong to. Every namespace the registry defines is placed in exactly one domain, and a
		test fails if a release adds one that is not. The flat list is still there, under{" "}
		<a href="/attributes">Registry</a>.
	</p>
</Shell>
